  CORE ARGUMENT: Hardimon reconstructs the Philosophy of Right around a "reconciliation thesis" -- the claim that the modern rational state is not alienating but constitutes the social world in which individuals can genuinely be at home. He analyzes how Hegel's three spheres of Sittlichkeit provide the institutional structure through which subjective freedom and objective social arrangements are reconciled rather than opposed.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the clearest account of Hegel's "at-homeness" thesis -- the idea that freedom as bei sich selbst requires not just institutional embeddedness but the specific institutions of modern ethical life. Important for contrasting Hegel's view with alienation-based critiques of the modern state.

  POSITION: Reconciliation reading; modern ethical institutions as genuinely hospitable to individual freedom.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, reconciliation, ethical-life, social-philosophy, High},
  abstract = {This book provides an authoritative account of Hegel's social philosophy at a level that presupposes no specialised knowledge of the subject. Hegel's social theory is designed to reconcile the individual with the modern social world. Michael Hardimon explores the concept of reconciliation in detail and discusses Hegel's views on the relationship between individuality and social membership, and on the family, civil society, and the state. The book is an important addition to the string of major studies of Hegel published by Cambridge. It will interest a broad swathe of readers in philosophy, (both students and specialists), and could be used in courses on political and social theory.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{patten2002idea,
  author = {Patten, Alan},
  title = {Hegel's Idea of Freedom},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  year = {2002},
  doi = {10.1093/0199251568.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Patten offers a systematic reconstruction of Hegel's concept of freedom, arguing that "freedom as rational self-determination" is the master concept that unifies the Philosophy of Right. He distinguishes Hegel's account from both liberal autonomy and communitarian embeddedness, showing that genuine self-determination requires both the subjective condition of rational self-legislation and the objective condition of actualization in social institutions.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a clear conceptual map of the structure of Hegelian freedom and is the key reference for comparing Hegel's positive liberty with Berlin's and with republican accounts. The book explicitly addresses the key question of how Hegel distinguishes genuine freedom from mere voluntarism.

  POSITION: Rational self-determination reading; Hegel as offering a third option beyond negative liberty and thick communitarianism.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, freedom, self-determination, rational-agency, High},
  abstract = {Abstract The book offers the first full‐length treatment in English of Hegel's idea of freedom. It explores his theory of what it is for an individual to be free and his account of the social and political contexts in which freedom is developed, realized, and sustained. The book investigates a number of central questions concerning Hegel's ethics and political theory. Is Hegel's outlook unacceptably conservative? Can freedom be equated with rational self‐determination? Is there any special connection between freedom and citizenship? By offering interpretations of Hegel's views on these and other questions, the book develops a novel ‘civic humanist’ reading of Hegel's social philosophy, one that restores to its proper, central place Hegel's idea of freedom. The book is written in a clear and jargon‐free style and will be of interest to anyone concerned with Hegel's ethical, social, and political thought and the sources of contemporary ideas about freedom, community, and the state.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{honneth2010pathologies,
  author = {Honneth, Axel},
  title = {The Pathologies of Individual Freedom},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  year = {2010},
  doi = {10.1515/9781400835027},
  abstract = {This is a penetrating reinterpretation and defense of Hegel's social theory as an alternative to reigning liberal notions of social justice. The eminent German philosopher Axel Honneth rereads Hegel's Philosophy of Right to show how it diagnoses the pathologies of the overcommitment to individual freedom that Honneth says underlies the ideas of Rawls and Habermas alike. Honneth argues that Hegel's theory contains an account of the psychological damage caused by placing too much emphasis on personal and moral freedom. Although these freedoms are crucial to the achievement of justice, they are insufficient and in themselves leave people vulnerable to loneliness, emptiness, and depression. Hegel argues that people must also find their freedom or "self-realization" through shared projects. Such projects involve the three institutions of ethical life--family, civil society, and the state--and provide the arena of a crucial third kind of freedom, which Honneth calls "communicative" freedom. A society is just only if it gives all of its members sufficient and equal opportunity to realize communicative freedom as well as personal and moral freedom.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Honneth argues that overemphasis on personal freedom (negative liberty) and moral freedom (Kantian autonomy) generates characteristic "pathologies" -- social isolation, lack of meaningful commitment, depression -- because both abstract from the relational and institutional conditions that make freedom psychologically real. Hegel's Sittlichkeit diagnoses and overcomes these pathologies by introducing a third mode of freedom, "communicative" or social freedom, realized in the shared projects of family, civil society, and state.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most influential contemporary reconstruction of Hegel's critique of abstract individualism and the argument that the institutions of ethical life are the cure for liberal pathologies. Directly relevant to comparing Hegel's positive liberty with Berlin's negative liberty.

  POSITION: Neo-Hegelian social-pathology reading; Sittlichkeit as normative corrective to liberal individualism.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, social-freedom, pathologies, sittlichkeit, High}
}

@book{honneth2014freedoms,
  author = {Honneth, Axel},
  title = {Freedom's Right},
  publisher = {Columbia University Press},
  year = {2014},
  doi = {10.7312/honn16246},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Honneth offers a systematic social philosophy reconstructing Hegel's Philosophy of Right as a normative theory of social freedom, arguing that just social institutions are those that actualize the three forms of freedom -- negative, reflexive, and social -- in the spheres of personal relationships, the market, and democratic will-formation. Social freedom, realized through mutual recognition in Sittlichkeit, is the integrating normative concept.

  RELEVANCE: The most developed neo-Hegelian account of how the institutions of ethical life (family, civil society, and state) are constitutive of rather than merely expressive of freedom, in direct dialogue with Rawlsian and deliberative-democratic alternatives. Crucial for questions about how Hegel's account differs from both liberal and republican theories.

  POSITION: Reconstructive Hegelianism; social freedom as normative criterion for a theory of justice grounded in the logic of recognition.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, social-freedom, sittlichkeit, recognition, freedom-right, High},
  abstract = {Axel Honneth constructs a theory of justice derived from the normative claims of Western liberal-democratic societies and anchored in morally legitimate laws and institutionally established practices. Honneth's paradigm demonstrates how concrete social spheres generate the principles of individual freedom and a standard for what is just. Using social analysis to re-found a more grounded theory of justice, he argues that all crucial actionsrequire the realization of a particular aspect of individual freedom.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{nance2016debates,
  author = {Nance, Michael},
  title = {Hegel's social and political philosophy: Recent debates},
  journal = {Philosophy Compass},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {11},
  number = {12},
  pages = {804--817},
  doi = {10.1111/phc3.12384},
  abstract = {This article discusses three topics that have been the subject of debate in recent scholarship on Hegel's social and political philosophy: first, the relevance of Hegel's systematic metaphysics for interpreting Hegel's social and political writings; second, the relation between recognition (Anerkennung), social institutions, and rational agency; and third, the connection between the constellation of institutions and norms that Hegel calls "ethical life" (Sittlichkeit) and Hegel's theory of freedom. This article provides a critical overview of the positions in these three debates. In the case of the first debate, I clarify the conceptual terrain by distinguishing between several kinds of systematicity that are at issue. In the case of the second debate, I argue that the views of two of the major participants, Axel Honneth and Robert Pippin, are in fact compatible. In the case of the third debate, I seek to clarify the connection in Hegel between two different ideas of freedom in ethical life, each of which has been emphasized by different interpreters of Hegel: the idea of freedom as non-alienation and the idea of freedom as social freedom. I conclude with a discussion of the ways in which ethical life, for Hegel, enables the freedom of individuals.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nance surveys three debates in recent Hegel scholarship -- the metaphysics debate (actualist vs. non-metaphysical readings), the recognition-agency debate (Honneth vs. Pippin), and the freedom debate (non-alienation vs. social freedom as the central concept in Sittlichkeit) -- and argues that the Honneth and Pippin positions are compatible rather than rival.

  RELEVANCE: The most useful single secondary source for orienting a research project on Hegel's freedom: maps the conceptual terrain of the field, identifies the key interpretive camps, and diagnoses the relationship between social freedom and non-alienation as the unresolved central issue.

  POSITION: Survey/synthetic; identifies the non-alienation vs. social-freedom distinction as the operative fault line in contemporary Hegel scholarship on freedom.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, freedom, sittlichkeit, survey, recognition, Medium}
}

@article{pippin1999naturalness,
  author = {Pippin, Robert B.},
  title = {Naturalness and Mindedness: Hegel's Compatibilism},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {1999},
  volume = {7},
  number = {2},
  pages = {194--212},
  doi = {10.1111/1468-0378.00081},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Pippin argues that Hegel is a compatibilist about freedom and natural causation, but that the relevant sense of freedom is not the ability to have done otherwise but rather the capacity for genuine self-determination through the actualization of one's rational nature in social life. Freedom requires not the absence of natural determination but rather the kind of self-determination that is possible for minded, social beings embedded in recognitive practices.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the metaphysical underpinning of Hegel's free will -- specifically, how freedom as Selbstbestimmung can be compatible with natural determination and how it differs from simple voluntarism. Important for the question of how Hegel's account relates to contemporary compatibilist debates.

  POSITION: Rational-agency reading; Hegelian compatibilism as grounded in the sociality of minded agency rather than in counterfactual ability.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, compatibilism, free-will, self-determination, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{james2012subjective,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {Subjective Freedom and Necessity in Hegel's Philosophy of Right},
  journal = {Theoria},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {59},
  number = {131},
  pages = {41--63},
  doi = {10.3167/th.2012.5913104},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James examines the tension between subjective freedom (the right of individuals to act on their own assessments and commitments) and the objective necessity of the institutions of Sittlichkeit in Hegel's Philosophy of Right. He argues that Hegel's resolution of this tension -- whereby subjective freedom finds its proper actualization within, rather than against, the rational institutions of ethical life -- distinguishes his account from both voluntarism and collectivism.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses the central question of how subjective and objective freedom are related in Hegel: how the sphere of Morality (subjective freedom) is preserved rather than cancelled in the transition to Sittlichkeit. Directly relevant to the "bei sich selbst im Anderen" theme.

  POSITION: Social-constitutive reading; subjective freedom as requiring, not conflicting with, objective ethical institutions.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, subjective-freedom, sittlichkeit, necessity, philosophy-of-right, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{yeomans2011freedom,
  author = {Yeomans, Christopher},
  title = {Freedom and Reflection: Hegel and the Logic of Agency},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  year = {2011},
  doi = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199794522.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Yeomans argues that Hegel's account of freedom is grounded in the logical structure of reflection as developed in the Science of Logic, and that practical freedom is best understood as a form of self-determinative reflection rather than as the absence of external constraint or the exercise of a prior will. The book traces the continuity between Hegel's logic of essence (Reflexionslogik) and his practical philosophy, showing that the free will is the logical structure of self-positing particularity within a universal.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most rigorous treatment of the logical underpinnings of Hegelian freedom -- specifically, the connection between the tripartite will-structure and Hegel's broader logical system. Important for understanding how the "three moments" of the will are not arbitrary but follow from the logic of self-determination.

  POSITION: Logical-foundational reading; freedom as realized reflection, connecting the Science of Logic to the Philosophy of Right.
  },
  keywords = {hegel, freedom, reflection, logic-of-agency, free-will, High},
  abstract = {While many interpreters hold that Hegel avoided the traditional problem of free will, Yeomans argues both that the problem is unavoidable, and that the two versions of the Logic fruitfully engage the tensions between explicability and both the control and alternate possibilities constitutive of free agency.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{tunick1992hegel,
  author    = {Tunick, Mark},
  title     = {Hegel's Political Philosophy},
  year      = {1992},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  doi       = {10.1515/9781400863075},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tunick examines whether Hegel's political philosophy,
  including his critique of liberal theories of rights and negative
  liberty, is coherent and defensible. He argues that Hegel's criticisms
  of liberalism are more nuanced than often presented — Hegel does not
  reject individual rights but recontextualizes them within a theory of
  ethical life in which rights require social recognition and
  institutional support to be effective.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the domain's question of whether
  Hegel's critique of abstract freedom entails a rejection of individual
  rights; offers a careful reading of the relevant passages in the
  Philosophy of Right.

  POSITION: Moderate reconstructive position that defends a form of
  Hegelian liberalism against the charge that Hegel's critique of
  negative liberty is politically illiberal.
  },
  keywords  = {Hegel, rights, political-philosophy, liberal, High},
  abstract = {To scholars of Western intellectual history Hegel is one of the most important of all political thinkers, but politicians and other "down-to-earth" persons see his speculative philosophy as far removed from their immediate concerns. Put off by his difficult terminology, many participants in practical politics may also believe that Hegel's idealism unduly legitimates the status quo. By examining his justification of legal punishment, this book introduces a Hegel quite different from these preconceptions: an acute critic of social practices. Mark Tunick draws on recently published but still untranslated lectures of Hegel's philosophy of right to take us to the core of Hegel's political thought. Hegel opposes radical criticism like that later offered by Marx, but, argues Tunick, he employs "immanent" criticism instead. For instance, Hegel claims that punishment is the criminal's right and makes the criminal free. From this standpoint, he defends specific features of the practice of punishment that accord with this retributive ideal and criticizes other features that contradict it. In a lucid account of what Hegel means by right and freedom, Tunick addresses Hegel specialists and those interested in criminal law, the interpretation of legal institutions and social practices, and justification from an immanent standpoint. Originally published in 1992. The Princeton Legacy Library uses the latest print-on-demand technology to again make available previously out-of-print books from the distinguished backlist of Princeton University Press. These editions preserve the original texts of these important books while presenting them in durable paperback and hardcover editions. The goal of the Princeton Legacy Library is to vastly increase access to the rich scholarly heritage found in the thousands of books published by Princeton University Press since its founding in 1905.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{franco1999hegel,
  author    = {Franco, Paul},
  title     = {Hegel's Philosophy of Freedom},
  year      = {1999},
  publisher = {Yale University Press},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Franco offers a comprehensive account of the
  development of Hegel's concept of freedom from the early theological
  writings through the mature Philosophy of Right. On the negative side,
  he traces Hegel's dissatisfaction with both Kantian formal morality
  and Rousseauvian general will as insufficient models, arguing that
  Hegel's critique of abstract freedom grounds the positive account
  of concrete freedom in the institutions of modern ethical life.

  RELEVANCE: Provides developmental context for the rejection of
  inadequate freedom conceptions, showing how Hegel's objections to
  abstract autonomy and negative liberty evolved; useful for tracing
  the intellectual background of the mature critique.

  POSITION: Historical-developmental reading that treats the rejections
  as philosophically motivated by Hegel's positive account of
  actualized freedom.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, freedom, political-philosophy, development, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{berlin1969four,
  author    = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  title     = {Four Essays on Liberty},
  year      = {1969},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Berlin's essay "Two Concepts of Liberty" defines
  negative liberty as the absence of interference by other persons and
  positive liberty as self-mastery or self-realization. Berlin argues
  that conflating these concepts — especially inflating positive liberty
  into a doctrine of rational self-realization — leads to political
  authoritarianism, a charge many readers apply to Hegel's account of
  freedom as self-determination in the rational state.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the contemporary analytic framework for the
  debate about negative liberty that Hegel's critics and defenders
  engage; any assessment of whether Hegel's critique of negative liberty
  is metaphysical, social, or political must reckon with Berlin's
  taxonomy.

  POSITION: Liberal defense of negative liberty and critique of
  positive-liberty traditions, including implicitly Hegelian
  self-realization theories.
  },
  keywords = {Berlin, negative-liberty, positive-liberty, liberalism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{stillman1974hegel,
  author    = {Stillman, Peter G.},
  title     = {Hegel's Critique of Liberal Theories of Rights},
  journal   = {American Political Science Review},
  year      = {1974},
  volume    = {68},
  number    = {3},
  pages     = {1086--1092},
  doi       = {10.2307/1959149},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stillman reconstructs Hegel's objections to liberal
  theories of rights, focusing on the claim that abstract, pre-social
  accounts of rights presuppose an atomistic conception of persons that
  is philosophically untenable: persons are constituted through social
  relations and cannot be the bearers of rights prior to and
  independent of social life.

  RELEVANCE: Article-length treatment directly addressing the domain's
  question of whether Hegel's critique of abstract freedom amounts to
  a rejection of individual rights; argues it does not, but that it
  requires a social-ontological grounding of rights.

  POSITION: Sympathetic reconstruction of Hegel's critique of liberal
  atomism while defending the compatibility of Hegelian theory with
  a robust account of individual rights.
  },
  keywords  = {Hegel, rights, liberalism, atomism, Medium},
  abstract = {In “Abstract Right,” the first part of the Philosophy of Right , Hegel criticizes the usual content and formulations of liberal theories of rights. In terms of content, Hegel argues that the subject of rights is only a narrow abstraction from the full human being; that he has limited self-determination and limited political freedom; and that, when he acts on his rights, he produces terror and destruction. In terms of formulations, Hegel argues that the pervasiveness of contract relations is inaccurate and undesirable; that the state cannot be derived from the natural man's alienating his right to punish; and that it is inaccurate to conceptualize civil society as only limiting natural man's freedoms. By transforming natural to abstract rights, Hegel retains much of the substance of rights, while concurrently preparing for the later sections of his text which try to overcome the inadequacies of a political theory based only on rights.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{finlayson1998does,
  author    = {Finlayson, Gordon},
  title     = {Does Hegel's Critique of Kant's Moral Theory Apply to Discourse Ethics?},
  journal   = {Bulletin of the Hegel Society of Great Britain},
  year      = {1998},
  volume    = {19},
  number    = {1-2},
  pages     = {17--34},
  doi       = {10.1017/s0263523200001269},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Finlayson examines whether Hegel's four main charges
  against Kantian moral theory — formalism, abstractness, the "empty
  formalism" of the categorical imperative, and the inability to
  motivate moral action — apply to Habermas's discourse ethics, which
  is a Kantian successor. He argues that some charges stick but that
  discourse ethics partially meets the Hegelian objections by
  grounding norms in intersubjective procedures.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a precise analytic decomposition of the structure
  of Hegel's critique of Kantian autonomy and formalism, distinguishing
  between charges that are purely internal to Kant's framework and those
  with broader force; useful for mapping the exact content of the
  objections to abstract moral autonomy.

  POSITION: Critical engagement with both Hegel and Habermas that
  takes Hegel's critique of formalism seriously while assessing its
  range.
  },
  keywords  = {Hegel, Kant, formalism, discourse-ethics, Moralitat, Medium},
  abstract = {Several years ago Jürgen Habermas wrote a short answer to the question: “Does Hegel's Critique of Kant apply to Discourse Ethics?” The gist of his short answer is, “no”. Insofar as Hegel's criticisms of the formalism and abstract universalism of the moral law never even applied to Kant's moral theory in the first place, they also fail to apply to discourse ethics. Insofar as Hegel's criticisms of the rigorism of the moral law and of Kant's conception of autonomy do hit the mark, discourse ethics successfully draws their sting by reconceiving Kant's moral standpoint along the following lines. 1. Kant wrongly undertakes to establish the moral law as a “fact of reason”: discourse ethics derives the moral standpoint from two premises — one formal, a rationally reconstructed logic of argumentation, and one material, namely our intuitions about how to justify utterances. 2. Kant wrongly contends that we must be able to think of ourselves as both intelligible characters, inhabiting a noumenal world, and as empirical characters inhabiting the world of appearances: discourse ethics allows that in everyday contexts of action and in the context of moral discourse we have one character that has real needs and interests. 3. Kant is also mistaken in arguing that moral autonomy requires human beings to abstract away from their needs and interests and to will universalizable maxims for the sake of their universal form: discourse ethics understands moral autonomy to consist in the free adoption of a standpoint from which conflicts of interest can be impartially regulated, by giving special weight to the satisfaction of universalizable interests. 4. Kant misconceives the categorical imperative as an objective test of universalizability that is applied by individual wills in isolation: discourse ethics reconceives the moral universalism as an ideal of intersubjective agreement of participants in discourse. On the differences between the principles of discourse ethics and Kant's categorical imperative Habermas is wont to cite McCarthy's summary of his — Habermas' — position: “Rather than ascribing as valid to all others any maxim that I can will to be a universal law, I must submit my maxim to all others for the purposes of discursively testing its claim to universality. The emphasis shifts from what each can will without contradiction to be a general law, to what all can will in agreement to be a universal norm” (MCCA 67).},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{saner2023embedded,
  author    = {Saner, Senem},
  title     = {Embedded agency: A critique of negative liberty and free markets},
  journal   = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year      = {2023},
  volume    = {51},
  number    = {1},
  pages     = {98--131},
  doi       = {10.1177/01914537231157439},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Saner argues that Berlin's defense of negative liberty
  leads to a paradox: freedom as non-interference, when applied to
  markets, produces subjection to the heteronomous necessity of
  self-regulating market forces. Drawing on Hegel's theory of
  self-determination, the paper argues that negative liberty reduces
  to Willkür — mere arbitrariness subject to external forces — and that
  genuine freedom requires embedded, intersubjectively mediated agency
  within social institutions.

  RELEVANCE: Recent application of Hegel's critique of negative liberty
  and Willkür to contemporary debates about freedom and markets;
  shows the continuing relevance of Hegel's objections and connects
  the metaphysical critique (negative liberty is inadequate) to social
  and political consequences.

  POSITION: Hegelian critique of negative liberty and market liberalism,
  arguing that social embedding is constitutive of rather than a
  constraint on genuine freedom.
  },
  keywords  = {Hegel, negative-liberty, Willkur, markets, social-freedom, Medium},
  abstract = {The concept of negative liberty as non-interference is operative in the concept of a free market and stipulates that market relations remain outside the purview of social control. As a purported self-regulating system, however, the market functions as a system of necessity that facilitates and rules social life. I argue that Isaiah Berlin’s defense of negative liberty leads to a paradox as it entails subjection to the external necessity of a self-regulating market. The argument for the self-defeating nature of negative liberty relies on two philosophical insights that have their roots in G. W. F. Hegel’s theory of self-determination. First, negative liberty fails to account for the inner and outer conditions of freedom and thus reduces to mere whim or arbitrariness, subject to heteronomous forces but masquerading as license. Second, individual freedom is intersubjectively mediated in its deliberative process and framed by social and political institutions in its exercise; thus, free agency must be theorized as embedded agency. I challenge the association of freedom and the market and the automatic and unchallenged implication of deregulation and freedom. Deregulation, rather, leaves the community’s future development to the whims of economic players using a rhetoric of freedom as both lure and principle.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{houlgate1995unity,
  author    = {Houlgate, Stephen},
  title     = {The Unity of Theoretical and Practical Spirit in Hegel's Concept of Freedom},
  journal   = {The Review of Metaphysics},
  year      = {1992},
  volume    = {46},
  number    = {4},
  pages     = {859--881},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Houlgate argues that Hegel's concept of genuine freedom
  requires the unity of theoretical and practical spirit: a will that
  is merely practical — choosing among given impulses (Willkür) — is
  unfree because it lacks the self-determination that comes only when
  the will is grounded in thought. Abstract moral autonomy (Kant) fails
  because it severs the practical from its concrete content; negative
  liberty fails because it abstracts from the thinking will altogether.

  RELEVANCE: Provides textual analysis of the Encyclopaedia passages
  on free spirit that ground Hegel's rejection of Willkür and abstract
  autonomy in his broader philosophy of spirit; essential for the
  domain's question about the philosophical basis of Hegel's objections.

  POSITION: Metaphysical-idealist reading that locates the critique of
  inadequate freedom concepts in Hegel's philosophy of spirit rather
  than solely in his social theory.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, Willkur, theoretical-practical, spirit, freedom, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{taylor1975hegel,
  author    = {Taylor, Charles},
  title     = {Hegel},
  year      = {1975},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address   = {Cambridge},
  doi       = {10.1017/cbo9781139171465},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Hegel's entire philosophical system through the
  lens of the expressivist tradition, arguing that Hegel's account of Geist
  articulates a social and historical conception of freedom as self-realization
  within rational institutions -- neither liberal atomism nor authoritarian
  statism.

  RELEVANCE: The foundational revisionist monograph for anglophone Hegel
  reception; establishes the communitarian reading that later informs Taylor's
  own debates with Rawlsian liberalism and shapes how Hegel's social freedom is
  appropriated by communitarians.

  POSITION: Expressivist communitarianism; Hegel as coherent philosopher of
  socially embedded freedom.
  },
  keywords  = {taylor, communitarianism, expressivism, social-freedom, High},
  abstract = {A major and comprehensive study of the philosophy of Hegel, his place in the history of ideas, and his continuing relevance and importance. Professor Taylor relates Hegel to the earlier history of philosophy and, more particularly, to the central intellectual and spiritual issues of his own time. He sees these in terms of a pervasive tension between the evolving ideals of individuality and self-realization on the one hand, and on the other a deeply-felt need to find significance in a wider community. Charles Taylor engages with Hegel sympathetically, on Hegel's own terms and, as the the subject demands, in detail. We are made to grasp the interconnections of the system without being overwhelmed or overawed by its technicality. We are shown its importance and its limitations, and are enabled to stand back from it.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{beiser2005hegel,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick},
  title = {Hegel},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  year = {2005},
  doi = {10.4324/9780203087053},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Offers a comprehensive account of Hegel's philosophy that resists neo-Kantian reductions and argues for the metaphysical ambition of Hegel's system, including his concept of absolute idealism and its bearing on the nature of freedom as spirit's self-realization.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the counter to Pippin's non-metaphysical reading, suggesting that freedom in Hegel is bound up with substantive claims about spirit, nature, and the Absolute — claims that span from the early writings to the Logic and beyond.

  POSITION: Defends a metaphysically robust reading of Hegel against neo-Kantian and pragmatist interpretations.
  },
  keywords = {hegel-metaphysics, absolute-idealism, historical-context, High},
  abstract = {Hegel (1770-1831) is one of the major philosophers of the nineteenth century. Many of the major philosophical movements of the twentieth century - from existentialism to analytic philosophy - grew out of reactions against Hegel. He is also one of the hardest philosophers to understand and his complex ideas, though rewarding, are often misunderstood. In this magisterial and lucid introduction, Frederick Beiser covers every major aspect of Hegel's thought. He places Hegel in the historical context of nineteenth-century Germany whilst clarifying the deep insights and originality of Hegel's philosophy.A masterpiece of clarity and scholarship, Hegel is both the ideal starting point for those coming to Hegel for the first time and essential reading for any student or scholar of nineteenth century philosophy.Additional features: glossary chapter summaries chronology annotated further reading.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{franco1999hegelphilosophy,
  author    = {Franco, Paul},
  title     = {Hegel's Philosophy of Freedom},
